{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014JmyVNnvSBtVxHu6dQzzLz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. I now have clear examples of the patterns used. Let me provide you with a comprehensive report:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've identified the architectural pattern used by this project's utilities. All three commands (cp, chmod, chown) follow a consistent structure. Here's what you need to know for implementing the install utility:\n\n### Key Pattern Elements\n\n**1. Main Entry Point Structure**\n- All call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at the very start of main()\n- Parse command-line arguments into flags and operands\n- Validate input and exit with code 1 on errors\n- Process files/operations with appropriate error handling\n- Exit with non-zero code if any operation fails\n\n**2. Flag Parsing Pattern**\n- Custom parseArgs/parseFlags function that manually iterates os.Args[1:]\n- Supports both short flags (-r, -i) and long flags (--recursive, --verbose)\n- Handles special cases: -- for end-of-flags, -t for target-directory with optional space\n- Returns tuple: (options struct, operand string(s), []string files)\n\n**3. Options/Config Pattern**\n- Struct type containing all boolean/string flag settings\n- Example from chown:\n  ```go\n  type options struct {\n      verbose       bool\n      changes       bool\n      silent        bool\n      reference     string\n      recursive     bool\n      noDereference bool\n      symlink       symlinkMode\n  }\n  ```\n\n**4. Error Handling Pattern**\n- Use custom error types when needed (cp uses errDeclined, errPartial)\n- Format errors with context (\"cannot stat\", \"cannot create\")\n- Report errors to stderr, return exit code 1 on failure\n- Silent mode (--quiet/-f) suppresses most errors\n\n**5. Operational Pattern**\n- Single-file operation function (copySingle, chownSingle)\n- Multiple-file operation function (copyMultiple, chownRecursive)\n- Recursive option triggers directory walking (filepath.WalkDir)\n- Preserve attributes using sys package helpers\n\n### Most Relevant File for Install\n\nThe **chown utility** (line 1-462 above) is most relevant to your install command because:\n1. Install needs to set file ownership (like chown)\n2. Install needs to set file permissions (like chmod)\n3. Chown demonstrates the recursive directory handling pattern with symlink options\n4. It shows how to use sys.Lstat/sys.Stat for file metadata\n\nThe structure you should follow for install:\n1. Parse flags (mode, owner:group, -d, -D, -g, -m, -o, -s, -v)\n2. Use options struct for configuration\n3. Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at start\n4. Handle source → destination copying (like cp)\n5. Apply ownership (like chown) and permissions (like chmod)\n6. Support recursive directory installation\n\nAll three utilities import and use the `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys` package for system operations that preserve metadata correctly."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa6fe2cc-2b21-4080-8876-027f7d7a88e3","uuid":"0af1fadb-2c1c-4255-b945-324ead1fc51f","timestamp":"2026-05-27T15:01:40.801Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5592/cmd/cp/main.go to understand the pattern used by file-copying utilities in this project. I need to see how they structure main(), parse flags, handle file copying, and call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(). Also check cmd/chmod/main.go or cmd/chown/main.go if they exist — these are relevant since install sets permissions and ownership. Report the full source of the most relevant one.","agentId":"a0fa881fea7648bd1","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. I now have clear examples of the patterns used. Let me provide you with a comprehensive report:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've identified the architectural pattern used by this project's utilities. All three commands (cp, chmod, chown) follow a consistent structure. Here's what you need to know for implementing the install utility:\n\n### Key Pattern Elements\n\n**1. Main Entry Point Structure**\n- All call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at the very start of main()\n- Parse command-line arguments into flags and operands\n- Validate input and exit with code 1 on errors\n- Process files/operations with appropriate error handling\n- Exit with non-zero code if any operation fails\n\n**2. Flag Parsing Pattern**\n- Custom parseArgs/parseFlags function that manually iterates os.Args[1:]\n- Supports both short flags (-r, -i) and long flags (--recursive, --verbose)\n- Handles special cases: -- for end-of-flags, -t for target-directory with optional space\n- Returns tuple: (options struct, operand string(s), []string files)\n\n**3. Options/Config Pattern**\n- Struct type containing all boolean/string flag settings\n- Example from chown:\n  ```go\n  type options struct {\n      verbose       bool\n      changes       bool\n      silent        bool\n      reference     string\n      recursive     bool\n      noDereference bool\n      symlink       symlinkMode\n  }\n  ```\n\n**4. Error Handling Pattern**\n- Use custom error types when needed (cp uses errDeclined, errPartial)\n- Format errors with context (\"cannot stat\", \"cannot create\")\n- Report errors to stderr, return exit code 1 on failure\n- Silent mode (--quiet/-f) suppresses most errors\n\n**5. Operational Pattern**\n- Single-file operation function (copySingle, chownSingle)\n- Multiple-file operation function (copyMultiple, chownRecursive)\n- Recursive option triggers directory walking (filepath.WalkDir)\n- Preserve attributes using sys package helpers\n\n### Most Relevant File for Install\n\nThe **chown utility** (line 1-462 above) is most relevant to your install command because:\n1. Install needs to set file ownership (like chown)\n2. Install needs to set file permissions (like chmod)\n3. Chown demonstrates the recursive directory handling pattern with symlink options\n4. It shows how to use sys.Lstat/sys.Stat for file metadata\n\nThe structure you should follow for install:\n1. Parse flags (mode, owner:group, -d, -D, -g, -m, -o, -s, -v)\n2. Use options struct for configuration\n3. Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at start\n4. Handle source → destination copying (like cp)\n5. Apply ownership (like chown) and permissions (like chmod)\n6. Support recursive directory installation\n\nAll three utilities import and use the `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys` package for system operations that preserve metadata correctly."}],"totalDurationMs":16173,"totalTokens":31433,"totalToolUseCount":5,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9001,"cache_read_input_tokens":21711,"output_tokens":714,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9001},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":7,"output_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":21711,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9001,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9001,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
